## 2026-07-30 11:38:13 +05:30 — Deleted-document re-upload fix

### Summary and root cause addressed

- Files modified: `backend/app/services/ingestion_jobs.py`,
  `backend/app/routes/ingestion.py`, `backend/app/routes/documents.py`, and
  `backend/tests/test_multitenant_architecture.py`.
- Re-upload failed after extraction, before chunk creation and vector indexing,
  because the worker tried to assign a deleted row's normalized hash to a new
  active placeholder while SQLite still enforced unconditional owner/hash
  uniqueness.
- The worker now resolves normalized content identity before embedding generation.
  Active identical content is rejected with `DOCUMENT_ALREADY_EXISTS`; compatible
  deleted content is deterministically selected by lowest content ID and safely
  reused; incompatible historical content follows normal ingestion.

### Implementation details

- Added one owner- and organization-scoped duplicate resolver with explicit
  `active_duplicate`, `active_reuse`, `deleted_reuse`, `deleted_rebuild`, and
  `new` outcomes.
- Deleted chunks are reused only when chunk count, ordered text, source type,
  embedding model, dimension, and stored vector shape are compatible.
- A deleted canonical content row is reactivated in the same `BEGIN IMMEDIATE`
  transaction that links the new version, removes its unused placeholder, and
  creates version-scoped chunks. Historical versions, chunks, files, audit rows,
  and workbook metadata remain intact.
- Reused embeddings receive new deterministic point IDs for the new document and
  version. Qdrant upserts are idempotent. Missing points are rebuilt on retry from
  persisted chunks without extraction or embedding regeneration.
- Missing or incompatible embeddings rebuild only the embedding/vector layer.
  A completed version is not exposed until vector upsert succeeds.
- Successful deleted-content reuse records `document.reupload.reused` and returns
  a safe result message. Active duplicate failures return
  `DOCUMENT_ALREADY_EXISTS`, are terminal, and retire the unused queued
  document/version/content placeholder without deleting history.
- Job reads, retry, and cancellation are strictly owner-scoped. Restore resolves
  identity using the document owner's scope and rejects a conflict instead of
  creating two active canonical rows.
- Failure logs include job, owner, document, version, stage, and safe error class;
  document text, hashes, SQL, paths, tokens, and provider internals are not
  returned to clients.

### Security and transaction considerations

- All content resolution and job operations use server-derived owner and
  organization identifiers and parameterized values. Client hashes are never
  trusted.
- Existing upload validation, parser limits, normalized-text calculation, and
  safety scanning remain in the canonical worker path.
- `BEGIN IMMEDIATE`, the active-only unique index, and explicit integrity-error
  classification provide the race boundary for simultaneous identical uploads.
- SQLite and Qdrant cannot share a transaction; durable processing state plus
  deterministic point IDs makes vector upsert retry-safe.

### Tests and verification

- Added regression coverage for delete/re-upload with the same and different
  names, active duplicates, different-content versioning, cross-user isolation,
  embedding/chunk reuse, missing-embedding rebuild, missing-vector recovery,
  repeated job execution, restart-safe indexes, migration conflicts, search, and
  chat citations.
- Added a TXT/PDF/DOCX/XLSX/PPTX/PNG lifecycle matrix. Existing parser tests
  continue to verify format-specific parsing and source provenance.
- Full backend result:
  `.venv312\Scripts\python.exe -m unittest discover -s tests -v` —
  82 tests passed in 271.224 seconds after the final owner-scope change.
- Search returned only the replacement active document, chat cited that document,
  active duplicate rejection remained terminal, and valid reuse did not call the
  embedding generator.

### Rollback notes and remaining risk

- Application rollback requires rolling back these worker/route changes together
  with the database index migration. Restore the pre-deployment database backup
  before starting the older application because the older startup code recreates
  obsolete indexes.
- Do not automatically delete retained files, historical content, or audit rows.
- Qdrant remains an external system without a transactional outbox. Retry-safe
  upserts cover ingestion failures, but delete/restore reconciliation should
  eventually receive durable operational tracking.

